sderby@garnet.berkeley.edu (Steve Derby) writes:

>- Instant death without warning. 
[snip]

Horrible. I don't mind nasty surprises (scroll of punishment, cursed stuff
in general, things rusting), I don't mind dying because of it. What I hate
is "X happens, nothing you can do, you die". Things like being zapped with
a wand of sleep in 3.1.0, and get slowly killed while asleep. Things like
stepping on a level teleport trap in 3.0 and ending up in heaven, the end.
Nothing you can do to prevent it, no warning, no escape. (These "features"
tend to disappear, probably because so many people complain about them)

>- Does anyone else think that wishes are *way* too easy to come by in the
>game? 
[snip]

*I* never get those wishes! More seriously, wishing for things is somewhat
of an anti climax. Finding, making or earning things is more fun. But NH game
balance requires that many wishes I guess.

>- Not to refuel the debate or anything, but wishing for quest artifacts? 
[snip]

Maybe wishes shouldn't be the free ride they are. If you wish for a 
powerful object, why should it just appear at your feet? Why shouldn't the
previous owner still be attached? You wish for Stormbringer, you get a 
master lich wielding it. I think this should depend on the power of the
item asked for; wishing for a scroll of enchant weapon should give it to
you, mayby with a few angry moster showing up that shout "Stop that 
thief". A wish for a quest artifact should probably dump you in the 
corresponding quest.

[snip]

Some things I don't like: 

- essential non obvious information. Like the potion and scroll needed to
  get beyond the castle. If you don't know it, you're stuck. If you do 
  know it's only a stupid formality. It adds nothing to the game except the 
  need to drag some objects around (and messages to r.g.h.). Similarly, I
  remember when the Medusa first appeared in Nethack. Almost all my Nethack
  friends considered it a farce: either you don't know about her, and *poef* 
  instant dead, or you do and with a blindfold/mirror you can walk right 
  past her.

- convergence of characters. All advanced characters tend to get very 
  similar. Gray dragon scale mail, speed boots, amulet of live saving, bag
  of holding, shield of reflection, all intrensics, all stats maxed, ring 
  of conflict, the best weapon artifact for the alignment, etc.

  There are a lot of ways to be powerful in nethack. Currently, winners 
  must use all, or nearly all of those ways. Because all classes start 
  with different abilities and items, and what you find is random, characters
  tend to become unique specimens. But then wishing, sacrificing, eating
  the right corpses, polypiling etc. are employed to make them all into the
  same "optimal" character.

  I think the game should be easier, but balanced such that you cannot have
  `everything'. This way all those demigods wouldn't be so similar. E.g.
  Armour vs. speed, warriors vs spellcasters, stealth vs fighting power.

- patience. Currently, the games favours those who exploit minor flaws
  in game balance endlessly to build a mega character. Maybe there should
  be some time penalty so that speed and daring is equally favoured.                                           

- spells. They are still fairly useless compared to wands and scrolls, or
  even a good weapon. A wizard should be making heavy use of spells
  in my opinion. Now they add little to the game.

>(By the way, before folks look at the above two comments and say I think the
>game is just too easy - I've never ascended - only made it a few levels down
>into Ghemmon with my best character.)

I never even reached Ghemmon. But I love nethack anyway.

